# Result (Measured)
# 1. No pageReady for a failed main-frame load, in every case:
#    - fresh WebView (A1–A3): first load 502; Retry with drop (-1); Retry with the node stopped (-6);
#    - over a page that had loaded (B, CRR-002 state with TWO onPageFinished calls per failed load): reload with 502, drop,
#      refused; Retry from an overlay over a loaded page with refused, then 502;
#    - Back into history (C) with 502, drop, refused: WebView loads the entry from the network and it fails.
#    Each time the overlay stayed (showWebDiagnostic, no showPage(overlay=null) until a pageReady).
# 2. pageReady exactly once per document for every successful load: Retry after recovery (A4, B, C), a new document
#    (location.href), reload, Back and history.forward()/back() (D3–D5).
# 3. Back-forward cache: not observed. Back b -> a re-loads document a (onPageStarted; the window marker set on a is gone;
#    navigation type back_forward) and the shell re-mounts and sends pageReady — with and without Cache-Control: no-store
#    on the documents (D3/D4 with no-store, D5 without).
# 4. The SPA rewrites /mobile?doc=x to /mobile/ after mount (router), so Back targets the rewritten entry; not relevant to the rule.
# 5. Not a "link blocked" case: the same-origin /rest/not-a-page link in B-link was allowed into the WebView and failed with
#    HTTP 404; it is a failed main-frame load over a loaded page all the same.
# Conclusion: the DS-006 premise holds on WebView 124; no Design Impact.
